  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2 over x, z from −2 to 2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss. It is 3 times steeper in x, so overshoot shows in x first: zigzag above learning rate 0.33, divergence above about 0.67.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with spread 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, so it is exactly zero at 64. A noisy step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Arrows are drawn at their true length in bowl units (scale 1). Orange is the mini-batch step the ball takes next. Grey is the full-data step at the same learning rate, with no noise.",
    "A run stops after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 6, the value at the bowl's steep edge (diverged). On screen the ball and trail are held at the bowl's edge, but the Loss readout shows the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed. Reset replays the same sequence. 'New run, keep last path' uses the next noise values and keeps the finished path as the faded ghost. The ball takes 5 steps per second at speed 1."
  ]
}